# A nappy that smells or puddles after a wash is a different answer from how to wash one: the
# fibres are coated, and stripping is what fixes it.
ENTRIES.append(entry(
    "nappy-smell",
    (("nappy", "nappies", "cloth nappies", "cloth nappy", "reusable nappies", "flat nappies",
      "prefold", "prefolds", "pocket nappies", "bamboo nappies", "nappy inserts"),
     ("smell", "smells", "smelly", "stink", "stinks", "stinking", "fusty", "absorbency",
      "absorbent", "not absorbing", "wont absorb", "wont hold", "stop absorbing", "strip",
      "stripping", "build up", "buildup", "residue", "conditioner", "stains", "stained",
      "grey", "dull", "leak", "leaking", "leaks", "puddles")),
    ("Smell that survives a wash and nappies that have stopped holding a puddle are almost "
     "always detergent or fabric conditioner built up in the fibres. Strip them once: a hot "
     "wash - 60C, or 90C for plain cotton prefolds - with no detergent at all and a tablespoon "
     "of soda crystals, then an extra rinse, and from then on no fabric conditioner and a "
     "smaller dose of powder. The other two causes are a wet bucket (a dry one with a lid is "
     "what keeps the smell down) and a main wash cooler than 60C, and a bag that sits for a "
     "week is a third. Line drying in sun takes the last of the stains out."),
    bias=2,
    guard=_a_leak_is_not_a_strip,
    avoid=("rash", "sore", "red bottom", "cream", "change my", "changing", "how many", "buy",
           "cost", "price", "brand", "nappy bin", "disposable", "still in", "potty")))

# The first eighteen months, where a card already existed for the question and none for the
# statement, and four questions with no card at all. Each names its own subject, so the second
# group is the child, the age or the ask and never a diagnosis.
ENTRIES.append(entry(
    # The id is the complaint said as the state, not the complaint: the health-visitor row above
    # already holds `baby-constipation`, and two entries under one id leave one of them answering
    # unseen (`tests.test_knowledge_load`). Both cards are reachable and they answer different
    # phrasings - "my baby is constipated" is the visit-today row, "my baby has no poo" and
    # "my babys poo is hard pellets" are this one - so neither is a copy to delete. That the two
    # read differently on the same complaint is #answers' question, not this rename's.
    "baby-hard-poo",
    (("constipated", "constipation", "hasnt pooed", "hasn't pooed", "not pooed", "not pooing",
      "hard poo", "hard poos", "straining", "wont poo", "won't poo", "no poo", "poo is hard",
      "pellet", "pellets"),
     # the child word is required: "how do i relieve constipation" is the adult's answer, and
     # the ask words alone (how do i, help) let a grown-up's line through
     # A son or a child can be ten and constipated, and this row is the first two years: the
     # words that stay are the ones only a baby is called, and the ages a baby is given.
     ("baby", "babies", "newborn", "infant", "weeks old", "month old", "months old",
      "breastfed", "formula fed", "my little one", "in a baby", "for a baby")),
    ("A baby who is feeding, passing wind and happy is rarely constipated, however often they "
     "go - a breastfed baby can go several days and be fine. If the poo is hard and pellet-like, "
     "offer cooled boiled water between feeds to a formula-fed baby, try bicycle legs and a "
     "clockwise rub of the tummy, and move on to pureed fruit once they are over four months. "
     "See the GP the same day if the tummy is hard and swollen, there is blood in the nappy, "
     "they are vomiting or feeding poorly, or a baby under six weeks has not passed a stool; "
     "never give a baby an adult laxative or a suppository."),
    bias=1,
    # an adult's constipation, a dog's, and a baby who is ill rather than bunged up. The
    # sickness words are named here rather than added to UNWELL_CHILD, because that tuple is
    # the sick-child card's own avoid and holding a card's own subject fences it off its line.
    avoid=("i am", "i've", "i have been", "myself", "my stomach", "ibs", "adult", "dog",
           "cat", "puppy", "kitten", "vet", "laxative", "vomiting", "vomited", "projectile",
           "green vomit", "blood in the", "throwing up", "been sick", "being sick") + UNWELL_CHILD,
))

# How often a baby goes, and what the colour means, is one of the commonest questions a new
# parent asks, and nothing owned it: "is it normal for a baby to poo 5 times a day" got the
# adult constipation card (more fibre, two litres of fluid a day), "is green poo normal in a
# baby" the sleeps-a-lot card, "how often should a baby poo" the bath. The complaint rows
# below are for a baby who is bunged up or unwell; this one is the parent asking whether what
# they found in the nappy is normal, so it says the range first and the red flags after.
ENTRIES.append(entry(
    "baby-poo-normal",
    (("poo", "poos", "pooed", "pooing", "poopy", "poo poo", "stool", "stools",
      "bowel movement", "bowel movements", "dirty nappy", "nappy contents",
      "poo colour", "poo is", "poos are"),
     ("baby", "babies", "newborn", "new born", "infant", "a baby", "in a baby",
      "for a baby", "weeks old", "week old", "month old", "months old", "breastfed",
      "formula fed", "formula-fed", "my little one")),
    ("Five or six poos a day is normal in a newborn, and so is once a week in a breastfed baby, "
     "as long as it stays soft and they are feeding and having six or more wet nappies a day. "
     "It should be mustard yellow; green usually only means it went through quickly, and the "
     "black-green first poos are normal for a day or two. Ring the health visitor or the GP "
     "today for white or very pale poo, blood, black poo after the first few days, watery poo "
     "that soaks the nappy, or a baby who feeds less, has a hard tummy, or is floppy or "
     "feverish."),
    # The rows that used to take the line are the bath ("how often" plus "baby" is 5 points,
    # the same as this row), the sleeps-a-lot card ("normal" plus "baby"), the adult
    # constipation row ("poo" plus "days") and the under-three-months dosing row ("how many"
    # plus the age). Nothing here fences them: on every one of those lines this row ties or
    # wins on score, so the bias carries it, and a bias is one number to read rather than four
    # other lanes' rows to edit. Old main's `change-a-nappy` keeps "how many wet nappies", and
    # the complaint rows keep a baby who is bunged up because of the avoid list below.
    bias=2,
    avoid=(# a baby who is bunged up, or hard and pellet-like poo, is the complaint rows' line
           "constipated", "constipation", "hasnt pooed", "hasn't pooed", "not pooed",
           "not pooing", "hard poo", "hard poos", "straining", "wont poo", "won't poo",
           "no poo", "pellets", "laxative",
           # changing one, buying them and the rash are other answers
           "change", "changing", "changed", "wipes", "nappy bin", "which nappies",
           "reusable", "cloth nappies", "how many nappies", "rash", "sore bottom",
           "red bottom", "red bum",
           # the washing, the potty and the grown-up's own poo are somebody else's question
           "washing machine", "stain", "stains", "soak", "potty", "toilet training",
           "toilet", "loo", "my poo", "my own", "myself", "adult", "dog", "puppy",
           "cat", "kitten", "vet", "hamster", "rabbit")))

